  "worked_solution": "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $\u03a0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\u27e80,3,1\u27e9\\times \u27e82,0,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =3i+2j-6k \\\\  & =\u27e83,2,-6\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $\u03a0$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e83,2,-6\u27e9 & =\u27e8-5,4,2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,2,-6\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e83,2,-6\u27e9 & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=\u27e83,1,5\u27e9+w\u27e8-2,-5,4\u27e9$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot \u27e83,2,-6\u27e9 & =-19 \\\\ \u27e83-2w,1-5w,5+4w\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,2,-6\u27e9 & =-19 \\\\ 3(3-2w)+2(1-5w)-6(5+4w) & =-19 \\\\ 9-6w+2-10w-30-24w & =-19 \\\\ -40w & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=0$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =\u27e83-2(0),1-5(0),5+4(0)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,1,5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(3,1,5).$",
